=== L4 Functional Areas and the Supply Chain ===
id: 3cc4b96d-3e40-4af2-b991-039b1d33ed65
slug: lesson-04
status: live tier:both updated:2026-07-30T22:28:59.255333+00:00
narration_manifest: array len 25

--- DESCRIPTION ---
How retail functional areas work together and how products move through the supply chain to customers.

--- CONTENT_HTML ---
<h2 data-narration-id="n1">Functional Areas in Retail</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n2">A retail business is made up of several <dfn class="term" data-def="Distinct departments within a retail business that each carry out a specific set of activities in order to keep the business running efficiently.">functional areas</dfn> — specialised departments that each take responsibility for a particular set of tasks. No single area operates in isolation; they must communicate and cooperate to meet customer needs and achieve the business's aims. In a large retailer such as a supermarket or department store, each area will have its own team and manager. In a small independent shop, one or two people may cover all areas at once.</p>

<h2 data-narration-id="n3">Key Functional Areas</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n4"><strong>Sales and customer service</strong> is the most visible part of a retail business. It covers everything from greeting customers in store to handling queries and complaints online. Staff here process transactions, provide product information and build relationships with shoppers. Because positive customer experiences drive repeat purchase and loyalty, this area directly affects revenue. <strong>Marketing</strong> works alongside sales by identifying target customers, promoting the product range through advertising and social media, and planning seasonal campaigns. Marketing informs the rest of the business about what customers want and what competitors are doing.</p>

<p data-narration-id="n5"><strong>Finance</strong> monitors all money flows — recording sales revenue, paying suppliers, managing payroll and producing profit and loss accounts. Finance sets the budgets that other departments must work within, so every functional area depends on it. <dfn class="term" data-def="The functional area responsible for recruiting, training, managing and motivating all employees within a retail business.">Human resources (HR)</dfn> looks after the workforce: recruiting staff, running induction and training programmes, managing rotas, dealing with disciplinary matters and ensuring the business complies with employment law. Good HR practice reduces staff turnover, which is especially important in retail where high turnover is common and expensive.</p>

<p data-narration-id="n6"><strong>Operations and logistics</strong> ensures that the physical side of the business runs smoothly — managing delivery schedules, coordinating transport, overseeing store maintenance and making sure the right systems and equipment are in place. Closely linked to this is <dfn class="term" data-def="The functional area that manages the flow, storage and display of products, including stock ordering, stock rotation and the physical organisation of the sales floor.">stock and merchandising</dfn>, which is responsible for ordering the right products in the right quantities, rotating stock so older items are sold first, organising the sales floor or online catalogue, and responding to changes in demand. In an online retailer, this area also manages the warehouse picking-and-packing operation.</p>

<div class="key-fact" data-narration-id="n7" data-revision-tip="Cover this and list each functional area from memory, giving one activity for each. The spec names: purchasing, logistics, warehousing, sales, customer service, marketing, finance, human resources, administration and ICT.">
  <div class="key-fact-label">Key Fact</div>
  <p>The functional areas of a retail business include: sales and customer service, marketing, finance, human resources, operations and logistics, stock and merchandising, purchasing, administration, and ICT. Each area has a distinct role, but they all rely on one another to keep the business running effectively.</p>
</div>

<h2 data-narration-id="n8">How Functional Areas Work Together</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n9">Functional areas are interdependent. When the marketing team runs a promotional campaign, it must communicate expected demand to stock and merchandising so that sufficient products are ordered in time. Finance must approve the campaign budget. HR may need to arrange extra staffing if a big sales event is planned. Operations and logistics must ensure deliveries arrive before the promotion goes live. A breakdown in communication between any of these areas can result in empty shelves, poor customer experiences or wasted money — all of which harm the business's aims.</p>

<div class="collapsible">
  <button class="collapsible-toggle" aria-expanded="false">
    <span>How do online retailers organise their functional areas differently?</span>
    <svg class="collapsible-icon" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/></svg>
  </button>
  <div class="collapsible-content"><div class="collapsible-inner">
    <p data-narration-id="n10">The functional areas are broadly the same for online and physical retailers, but their relative size and activities differ. An e-commerce retailer such as ASOS places enormous emphasis on its ICT department to maintain the website, app and payment systems. Its customer service area handles queries entirely via live chat, email and social media rather than face-to-face. Logistics becomes critical because every sale requires picking, packing and dispatching a parcel — the warehouse is effectively the "shop floor". Merchandising shifts to digital product photography, search-engine optimisation and website layout rather than physical shelf arrangement. A physical store, by contrast, invests more in visual merchandising and in-store staffing. In a hybrid multi-channel retailer, both sets of activities are needed simultaneously, requiring careful coordination between the physical and digital sides of each functional area.</p>
  </div></div>
</div>

<h2 data-narration-id="n11">The Retail Supply Chain</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n12">The <dfn class="term" data-def="The sequence of steps through which a product passes — from raw materials and manufacturing through to the final consumer, including sourcing, logistics and stock control.">supply chain</dfn> describes how products move from their point of origin to the end customer. The classic retail supply chain has four stages: <strong>manufacturer → wholesaler or distributor → retailer → consumer</strong>. A manufacturer produces the goods. A <strong>wholesaler or distributor</strong> buys in large quantities from manufacturers, stores them in large warehouses and sells smaller batches to retailers. The <strong>retailer</strong> buys from the wholesaler, displays products in a store or online and sells them to the <strong>consumer</strong>. Some large retailers — including major supermarket chains — cut out the wholesaler entirely by buying direct from manufacturers in huge volumes, which lowers costs but requires significant warehousing capacity.</p>

<h2 data-narration-id="n13">Sourcing</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n14">Sourcing is the process of finding and selecting suppliers. When choosing a supplier, a retailer must consider <strong>cost</strong> (the price per unit, minimum order quantities and payment terms), <strong>quality</strong> (whether the product meets the standard customers expect), <strong>delivery time and reliability</strong> (a supplier who delivers late or inconsistently causes stock-outs and dissatisfied customers) and <strong>ethical and environmental factors</strong> (whether workers are paid fairly, whether production damages the environment and whether the retailer's brand image could be harmed by association with an unethical supplier). Sourcing decisions directly affect a retailer's profit margin and its reputation.</p>

<div class="key-fact" data-narration-id="n15" data-revision-tip="Remember the three stages of the supply chain: sourcing, logistics and stock control. For sourcing, the key criteria are cost, quality, delivery time and reliability, and ethical/environmental factors.">
  <div class="key-fact-label">Key Fact</div>
  <p>The three stages of the retail supply chain are: sourcing (finding suppliers), logistics (transporting and storing products) and stock control (managing stock levels, recording movements and maintaining supply). All three stages must be managed effectively for products to reach customers at the right time, in the right condition and at a competitive cost.</p>
</div>

<h2 data-narration-id="n16">Logistics and Warehousing</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n17">Logistics covers everything involved in physically moving products: <strong>transport from source</strong> (by road, rail, sea or air depending on the product and distance), <strong>storage or warehousing</strong>, <strong>packaging</strong> (protecting goods and meeting legal labelling requirements), <strong>security</strong> (preventing theft or damage in transit) and <strong>distribution to the customer</strong> (including home delivery for online orders). <strong>Warehousing</strong> is a core part of logistics. Large distribution centres receive stock in bulk from suppliers, sort and store it, then send it out to individual stores or directly to online customers. Efficient warehousing reduces the time between a customer placing an order and receiving it.</p>

<h2 data-narration-id="n18">Stock Control</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n19">Stock control is the system a retailer uses to make sure it always has the right amount of stock. Key activities include: <strong>having sufficient stock</strong> to meet demand without over-ordering, <strong>recording stock and stock movements</strong> accurately, <strong>checking stock levels</strong> regularly against recorded figures, <strong>re-ordering stock</strong> when levels fall to a minimum threshold (sometimes called the re-order level), <strong>rotating stock</strong> so older items are used or sold first (especially important for perishable goods), and <strong>dealing with damaged, returned and out-of-date stock</strong> in a way that minimises waste and loss. Different types of stock require different storage conditions — frozen foods need refrigeration, high-value items such as jewellery need secure display cases, and fragile items need careful handling to avoid breakage.</p>

<div class="collapsible">
  <button class="collapsible-toggle" aria-expanded="false">
    <span>How does technology improve stock control?</span>
    <svg class="collapsible-icon" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/></svg>
  </button>
  <div class="collapsible-content"><div class="collapsible-inner">
    <p data-narration-id="n20">Technology has transformed stock control in retail. <strong>EPOS (Electronic Point of Sale)</strong> systems automatically update stock levels every time an item is scanned through the till, giving managers a real-time picture of what is selling and what remains. <strong>Computerised stock management systems and databases</strong> track stock movements from delivery to sale and flag items that are running low. <strong>Automated ordering</strong> systems can place a purchase order with a supplier the moment stock drops below the re-order level, without any human intervention — reducing the risk of stock-outs and saving staff time. The benefits of using technology in stock control include greater accuracy (less human error in counting), reduced waste (especially for perishable goods), lower storage costs (less need to hold large buffers of safety stock) and improved availability for customers.</p>
  </div></div>
</div>

--- CONCLUSION_HTML ---
<h3 data-narration-id="n22">Key Takeaways</h3>
<ul>
  <li data-narration-id="n23">A retail business is divided into functional areas including sales and customer service, marketing, finance, HR, operations and logistics, and stock and merchandising. Each area has specific responsibilities but all depend on one another to deliver a good customer experience.</li>
  <li data-narration-id="n24">The retail supply chain moves products from manufacturer to wholesaler or distributor to retailer to consumer. Large retailers may buy direct from manufacturers to cut costs.</li>
  <li data-narration-id="n25">The three stages of supply chain management are sourcing (selecting suppliers on cost, quality, reliability and ethics), logistics (transport, warehousing, packaging, security and distribution) and stock control (recording, rotating, re-ordering and handling damaged or out-of-date stock).</li>
</ul>

--- EXAM_TIP_HTML ---
<p data-narration-id="n21">Questions on functional areas often present a retail scenario and ask you to identify which area is responsible or to explain how two areas must work together. Always name the specific functional area and link it to a business outcome — for example, "The HR department recruits and trains staff, which ensures the sales team can provide high-quality customer service, helping the retailer retain customers." For supply chain questions, remember the three stages (sourcing, logistics, stock control) and be ready to apply each to a given product type. For example, perishable stock requires faster logistics and strict rotation to prevent waste; high-value stock requires tighter security in logistics and storage.</p>

