  "text": "A fashion retailer is planning a summer sale campaign. The marketing department has designed a promotion offering 30% off selected lines for one week.\n\nDiscuss how two other functional areas will need to work with the marketing department to ensure the campaign is successful.",
  "type": "6 marks — Discuss",
  "marks": "Mastering (5-6 marks): A developed discussion of two functional areas with clear explanation of their role and how they interact with marketing. For example: stock and merchandising must be informed about which product lines are featured so that sufficient stock is ordered in advance and the sales floor is arranged to highlight the promoted items — if stock runs out in the first day the promotion damages customer trust. Finance must approve the budget for the campaign and for any extra stock purchased, and must monitor revenue against cost to ensure the sale still generates profit rather than a loss. A high-level answer notes interdependence between all three areas and the risk of campaign failure if communication breaks down. Secure (3-4 marks): Two functional areas identified with brief explanation of each. Developing (2 marks): One functional area with some explanation. Emerging (1 mark): A basic point with no development. [6 marks]"
 },
 {
  "text": "A national supermarket chain is reviewing its supply chain. It currently uses a wholesaler but is considering buying direct from manufacturers.\n\nEvaluate whether buying direct from manufacturers would be beneficial for the supermarket.",
  "type": "8 marks — Evaluate (Extended Response)",
  "marks": "Mastering (7-8 marks): A balanced evaluation of arguments for and against. For: cutting out the wholesaler removes an intermediary margin, lowering the cost per unit and improving profitability or allowing lower shelf prices; the retailer has greater control over quality standards and can build direct relationships with manufacturers. Against: the supermarket must now handle all large-scale warehousing itself, which requires significant capital investment in distribution centres; the buying team needs greater expertise in managing a more complex supplier network; minimum order quantities from manufacturers may be very large, tying up cash in stock. Substantiated judgement — e.g. for a national supermarket with scale and capital this is likely beneficial overall, but a smaller regional chain may lack the resources to manage it effectively. Secure (5-6 marks): At least two arguments on each side, some applied, an attempt at a judgement. Developing (3-4 marks): Points largely one-sided or lacking application. Emerging (1-2 marks): Basic points with no evaluation. [8 marks]"
 }
]

